[FINAL-TEXT] === DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: true
faulty_devices: ispa, ispb
diagnosis_text:
- Affected component: Upstream routing (default gateway) on the simulated ISP edge devices.
- Mechanism: PC1’s traffic is correctly routed through the campus to r1 and NATed, but when r1 forwards it to the ISP, the ISP routers send it back toward r1 due to a wrong default route. This creates a routing loop and results in timeouts to any public IP.
- Localization: LAN is healthy (pc1 can reach its gateway 10.1.0.1; intra-office reachability ok). r1 is healthy and can reach both ISP peers (r1→203.0.113.1 and →203.0.113.5 succeed) and has NAT enabled (MASQUERADE on eth2/eth3; ip_forward=1). However, r1→8.8.8.8 fails with “ICMP Redirect Host (New nexthop: 203.0.113.2)” from 203.0.113.1. On ispa, ip route shows “default via 203.0.113.2 dev eth0” (the customer r1), and its own ping to 8.8.8.8 gets “Redirect Host (New nexthop: 203.0.113.1)” from r1. On ispb, ip route shows “default via 203.0.113.6 dev eth0” (also r1), and ping to 8.8.8.8 returns TTL exceeded from 192.0.2.1, indicating a loop across the ISP interlink.
- Root cause: Both ISP edge routers (ispa, ispb) have their default route misconfigured to point back to the customer router (r1) instead of to an upstream internet next hop.
- Corrective fix: Remove the incorrect default routes on ispa and ispb and install the proper default toward their real upstream (e.g., via their inter-ISP/core-facing interfaces, not 203.0.113.2/6). After correcting the ISP default(s), verify r1 can ping 8.8.8.8 and that PC1 can load external sites. No reboot of on-prem devices is required.

=== END ===